# The published LIDAR heights, as a *source*.
#
# One `root datasource` over the parquets of the cities that publish a
# `height_m` (the ones importing tree_height.preql; docs/TREE_HEIGHT.md),
# mapping `tree_id` and `height_m` and nothing else. Root means unmanaged:
# the predictions job reads the heights and never rebuilds a city.
#
# Why not the rollup: `full_tree_publish.preql` reads all the city parquets
# as one multi-file scan, which binds its schema from the first file, so a
# column only some cities carry cannot be selected there without adding it
# to every city first (the crown-width outage;
# docs/CROWN_WIDTH_ROLLOUT_FAILURES_HANDOFF.md). This file lists only the
# cities that have the column. Adding a height city is one more entry in the
# file list; test_tree_height.py checks the list against the importers.
#
# Freshness needs no edge of its own: a city rebuild republishes the rollup,
# and the rollup's publication is one of the predictions job's watermarks.
#
# `tree_id: ~tree_id`: these files hold a fraction of the rollup's trees,
# which is a partial key on a complete table -- the planner pads the join and
# a tree with no height row keeps its other columns. Never a `partial
# datasource` (memory: pytrilogy-0401-partial-only-regression).

import tree_common;

property tree_id.height_m float; # tree height in metres from a LIDAR canopy survey, as the city published it; null where the tree is under no surveyed crown

root datasource published_tree_heights (
    tree_id: ~tree_id,
    height_m: ?height_m,
)
grain (tree_id)
file [
    f`https://storage.googleapis.com/trilogy_public_models/duckdb/trees/usbos_tree_info_v{data_version}.parquet`,
];
